## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence confirms that NTA did make an error in the NEET 2020 results: Mridul Rawat was initially declared as failed (with 329/720 marks) on the first marksheet, but after rechecking, his score was corrected to 650/720, and he emerged as the All India Topper in the **Scheduled Tribe (ST) category**. However, the overall All India toppers were Soyeb Aftab and Akanksha Singh, who both scored a perfect 720/720. The claim states that "the all India topper had failed," which conflates the ST category topper with the overall all-India topper—a significant distinction.

## Missing Information

No further information is needed; the evidence is sufficient to make a decision.

## Conclusion

The claim is technically true in that NTA did erroneously declare a topper as failed, but it is misleading because it omits the crucial context that the affected student was the **ST category topper**, not the overall **all-India topper**. The overall all-India toppers (Soyeb Aftab and Akanksha Singh) scored a perfect 720/720 and were never declared as failed. By omitting this important distinction, the claim creates a significantly different impression of what occurred.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The fact-check confirms that NTA did make an error in the NEET 2020 results: Mridul Rawat was initially declared as failed with 329/720 on the first marksheet, but after rechecking, his score was corrected to 650/720, revealing him as the All India Topper in the **Scheduled Tribe (ST) category** ([Livemint](https://www.livemint.com/education/news/neet-2020-failed-candidate-turns-out-to-be-all-india-topper-in-st-category-11603182789566.html), [India.com](https://www.india.com/education/neet-2020-result-blunder-aspirant-declared-failed-emerges-as-topper-deets-here-4179014/)). However, the overall All India toppers were Soyeb Aftab and Akanksha Singh, who both scored a perfect 720/720 and were never declared as failed ([OneIndia](https://www.oneindia.com/india/neet-2020-when-a-topper-was-declared-failed-3165796.html)). The claim is therefore misleading because it conflates the ST category topper with the overall all-India topper, omitting the crucial contextual distinction and creating a significantly different impression of the incident, warranting a **cherry-picking** verdict.